# Vigil Open AI Agent Security Benchmarks

This document is a status and methodology register, not a certification and not a claim of universal protection.

## Current result status

**No numerical result is currently independently reproducible from the files in this repository.**

The prior result matrix showing 97.4% direct-injection TPR, 94.8% indirect-injection TPR, 0.6% FPR, and 11ms p95 latency is withdrawn from current marketing use pending publication of the dataset, scripts, pinned environment, detector commit, and raw results. The earlier statement that this repository contained 500+ curated cases is also unverified against the current repository tree.

A separate internal audit report describes 47.0% attack detection at 1.0% FPR for a hybrid PromptShield evaluation and 22.4% at 0.22% FPR for deterministic mode. Those figures have not been reproduced from this repository and must not be represented as repository-verified results.

See docs/REPRODUCIBILITY-STATUS.md before using any performance number externally.

## Benchmark scope

The intended evaluation covers:
- Direct and indirect prompt injection.
- Text, encoding, Unicode, markup, document and supported multimodal obfuscation.
- Multilingual, cross-script, transliterated and code-switched content.
- RAG/memory poisoning and multi-turn propagation.
- Tool/MCP poisoning, schema drift, shadowing, rug pulls and unsafe arguments.
- Data exfiltration, privilege escalation, and resource/cost abuse.

See docs/ATTACK-TAXONOMY.md and docs/GLOBAL-LANGUAGE-SECURITY.md.

## Required metrics

- TPR/recall, precision, FPR, FNR.
- Per-language and per-attack-family confusion matrices.
- p50/p95/p99 latency, split by deterministic, classifier-assisted and end-to-end path.
- Confidence intervals, sample counts, dataset hash, detector commit, model/provider version, hardware and run configuration.

## Known limitations

- Text-only scanning cannot establish that image/audio/video or hidden document layers are safe unless the corresponding OCR/ASR/document parser is actually integrated and tested.
- Encoding and obfuscation detection is bounded by supported decoders and resource limits.
- Pattern matching is a detection layer, not an authorization boundary.
- Runtime protection must also enforce least privilege, tool argument policy, human approval for consequential actions, output validation, and egress controls.
